65,3,1,talk,Mio,,Option A seems the safest for our budget since transport is so expensive! But Option C sounds like a blast! I'm just worried about those non-refundable deposits if we go for the lodge.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Option C sounds lovely. Having our own building would allow the members to relax and bond in the evenings, which is exactly what we need to bring the different year-groups together.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option B is out of the question. The municipal reservation system is a bureaucratic nightmare, and shared dormitories often imply self-catering. We cannot risk the organizers spending their entire camp on duty again.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Rin,,"I agree with Ryuji! Option B is a no-go. Shared dorms usually mean being crammed together, and I bet it's just like those inland places where you can't even move in your sleep!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,1,talk,Misaki,,Option A is problematic because separate accommodation for commuters creates unpredictable costs. Option C is too risky; a new venue with a non-refundable deposit could easily lead to another financial deficit.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Mio,,"But if we pick Option A, won't the people staying in hotels feel left out? I want everyone to feel equal! Maybe we can find a way to make the lodge's deposit work?","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"Mio makes a very thoughtful point. If we separate the members by where they sleep, we risk further dividing the year-groups. A shared home at the lodge would truly unite us.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,"Option C is the most logical choice for the organizers. The owner handles the meals, which eliminates the cooking burden, and it avoids the bureaucratic mess of the municipal system. It is the only sustainable option.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Rin,,"I'm with Ryuji! A guesthouse sounds way more comfortable than a dorm. If we have our own building, we can actually get some real sleep and not wake up feeling like zombies!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"Comfort and convenience are secondary to financial certainty. Option C is a new venue; without a proven track record of costs, we are gambling with the club's remaining funds. We cannot risk another deficit.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Mio,,"I see Misaki's point, but Option A's separate costs are also unpredictable! If we go with Option C, we just need a strict rule about cancellations so the remaining members aren't stuck with the bill. That's fair, right?","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"I believe that is a fair compromise. If we can secure the finances, the Coastal Lodge offers the best chance for our members to truly connect and feel like a family again.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"Exactly. A strict cancellation policy solves the financial risk, while the lodge solves the labor risk. We cannot return to the chaos of self-catering or bureaucratic lotteries. Option C is the only choice that protects the organizers' time.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Rin,,"And it protects our sleep! If we go to the lodge, we won't be squeezed eight to a room. I'm voting for Option C so we can actually wake up feeling energized and happy!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"A strict policy is not a guarantee of payment. If members cannot pay, the club account will again bear the burden. However, Option A is equally unstable. I will vote for Option C, provided the price is fixed.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Mio,,"I'm so glad we all agree! Let's go with Option C. As long as we're clear about the costs and the cancellation rules, it'll be the most fun and fair trip ever! I vote for Option C!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Shizue,,I agree. The opportunity to foster genuine friendships across all year-groups is far too precious to pass up. I believe the Coastal Lodge is the right choice for our club's heart. I vote for Option C!,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,Option C is the only choice that ensures the organizers can actually participate in the training. It removes the administrative and labor burdens that plagued us last year. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Rin,,I'm so happy we're doing this! No more sardine-rooms or zombie-days for us. Let's go on a real adventure and get some great sleep! I vote for Option C!,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
84,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"I still have reservations about the financial risk, but Option A is too fragmented to be viable. If we ensure the owner provides a fixed, all-inclusive quote, I will accept it. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
